NetHack is not Xfree86. Are we comparing a bass boat to a carrier
group?

One of the obvious purposes of autotools is to make specifying build
and installation options simpler and less surprising. Installing
NetHack (or whatever) into a non-standard location should be as simple
as ./configure --prefix=/not/usr/local/include, regardless of the
platform used.

Perl's configure script is widely considered an abortion. Nowadays
there is little reason to do anything than configure -d unless you're
doing test builds. I'm surprised that anyone ever gets Perl built
right.

Using a confusing build process as an IQ test that weeds out the non
l33t is comprehensively wrongheaded. I take pride in building software
that does what people want, as directly and unsurprisingly as
possible. When someone wants to "just install something," the right
thing for the build system to do is just that. If it can't, that's a
problem with the developer and the software, not the user.



